<!--
  Type scale and spacing scale.

  Before this file, FontSize was a scattered inline literal at every call site - 10, 10.5, 11,
  11.5, 12, 13, 14, 18, 26 all appeared somewhere, several of them ("10.5", "11.5") existing only
  because two nearby call sites had each picked a slightly different round number for what was
  meant to be the same "small label" or "body text" role. There was no way to tell, from a given
  FontSize="11.5", whether that size was load-bearing or just drift.

  This collapses that down to seven named steps and a five-step spacing scale (base unit 4, the
  same unit the existing Metro geometry already uses informally - button height 22, row padding
  8/3, tree indent 14 - so the scale documents an existing rhythm rather than inventing a new
  one). Not every existing literal maps onto a clean multiple of 4 (the 22px button height and
  14px tree indent both come from ported-verbatim WPF ControlTemplate measurements - see
  MetroStyles.axaml's header comment - and are left as-is rather than rounded, since they are
  fidelity to the original, not drift).

  These are theme-invariant (a font size doesn't change between light and dark), so they live
  outside MetroDark.axaml's <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries> - see that file's header for
  why colour and typography are split into separate dictionaries.

  Density: every x:Double/Thickness below is a fallback value only - the one this file's XAML
  parses to at design time and whatever a screen renders before Services/AppSettings.cs's static
  constructor has run. Services/DisplayDensity.cs overwrites every one of these keys (by name, at
  the Application.Resources level) the moment the app starts, and again on every density change a
  user makes afterwards; see that file's header for exactly how a plain resource-dictionary
  mutation reaches every DynamicResource consumer live, and for which consumers it *cannot* reach
  (anything with a locally-set value, which rules out most of the field-table/tag-tree row
  content - that gap is spelled out there, not repeated here). The values below are the "Default"
  density tier specifically - DisplayDensity.MetricsFor's own "Default" case is required to match
  them exactly, so picking that tier at runtime is a no-op against whatever ships in this file.
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!-- ============================== Type scale ==============================
       Usage guide:
         Micro  - timestamps, badge/chip text, offsets in the field table
         Small  - secondary labels, section headers, tree rows
         Base   - default body text (TextBlock's own default already matches this)
         Medium - emphasised inline text (tag document header title)
         Large  - dialog/panel field headers
         Title  - the ASSEMBLY wordmark
         Display- the empty-state headline                                        -->
  <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeMicro">10</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeSmall">11</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeBase">12</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeMedium">13</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeLarge">15</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeTitle">18</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeDisplay">26</x:Double>

  <!-- ============================== Spacing scale ==============================
       Base unit 4px. Used for Margin/Spacing/Padding in the chrome this pass owns (header
       band, toolbar, tab strip, console, status bar, empty state). Kept as plain x:Double
       rather than Thickness, since most call sites need it as one side of an asymmetric
       Thickness or as a StackPanel.Spacing, not a uniform four-sided inset. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="Space1">4</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space2">8</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space3">12</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space4">16</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space5">20</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space6">24</x:Double>

  <!-- ============================== Density-driven control geometry ==============================
       Consumed from MetroStyles.axaml's Button/ToggleButton/TextBox/ComboBox/ListBoxItem/
       ListBox#FieldList/ToggleButton.blockExpander/ToggleButton.chrome Setters (search each key
       there) instead of each one's old separate literal, so a density change is one write per key
       here rather than a dozen scattered ones. Deliberately NOT every geometry constant in the
       app - Button.tabHeader's Height stays a fixed 30 (see its own comment in MetroStyles.axaml)
       because the tab strip's containing Grid row is a fixed pixel height outside this pass's
       ownership, and scaling one without the other would clip or float the tab buttons instead of
       actually changing anything. TreeViewItemMinHeight and TreeViewItemExpandCollapseChevronSize
       are declared in MetroStyles.axaml's own <Styles.Resources> instead of here - they override
       two of Avalonia's Fluent TreeViewItem ControlTheme's own resource keys, by name, and that
       override has to live where it already does (see that file's "Tree" section) - but
       DisplayDensity.Apply writes to those same key names too, shadowing whichever tier that
       section currently declares, exactly as it does for every key below. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="ControlHeight">22</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="ChromeToggleWidth">26</x:Double>
  <Thickness x:Key="ListBoxItemPadding">8,3</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="FieldRowPadding">0,3,8,3</Thickness>
  <x:Double x:Key="BlockExpanderSize">16</x:Double>
  <Thickness x:Key="TypeBadgePadding">4,1</Thickness>

</ResourceDictionary>
